        "heading": "Eight good code names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, one or two from each style:</p><ul><li><strong>Agent Nightjar</strong>: a nightjar is a bird that hunts at dusk and is hard to spot by day, which suits a spy.</li><li><strong>Glass Needle</strong>: two plain words that sound like a secret without saying what the secret is.</li><li><strong>Operation Rookery</strong>: one old word, easy to say over a radio and with nothing in it about the plan.</li><li><strong>Operation Northern Drawbridge</strong>: the two-word style many readers know from war films.</li><li><strong>Project Bluebell</strong>: friendly enough for a work project that everyone will say in meetings for months.</li><li><strong>Tinderbox</strong>: a one-word code name with a hint of something about to catch.</li><li><strong>Echo Seven</strong>: a call sign built from the NATO alphabet and a number.</li><li><strong>Agent Wobbly Marshmallow</strong>: for a secret agent aged about seven.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How code names work",
        "content": "<p>A code name has one job: to let people talk about something without telling an eavesdropper what it is. So the best ones are ordinary words with no link to the real thing. A project to replace the office printers should not be called Project Ink.</p><p>In a 1943 note usually credited to Winston Churchill, wartime planners were told to avoid boastful or silly operation names, because no family should hear that their son was lost in an operation called \"Bunnyhug\" or \"Ballyhoo\". British operation names are still meant to give nothing away, which is why so many are single, slightly old-fashioned words.</p><p>Software and product teams borrowed the habit. Their code names keep a release secret until launch and give a project a name before the marketing team has chosen the real one. Many teams pick a theme (birds, stones, spices) and work through it, which is what the Project and One word styles here do.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Call signs and the NATO alphabet",
        "content": "<p>The NATO spelling alphabet (Alpha, Bravo, Charlie and so on) was adopted in 1956 so that letters could be read out clearly over a crackly radio. Call signs join a letter word to a number: Echo Seven, Sierra Three, Tango Seven-Eight. The official spellings are actually Alfa and Juliett, so that people who don't speak English say them correctly; this generator uses the everyday spellings.</p><p>Two letter words together can spell out an abbreviation by accident, and some of those are rude, so the NATO style here always pairs one letter word with numbers.</p>"
