        "heading": "Choosing one that works",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Say it out loud.</strong> A code name gets shouted across rooms and typed into chats. If you have to spell it twice, pick another.</li><li><strong>Keep it unconnected.</strong> If people can guess the secret from the name, it is a label, not a code name.</li><li><strong>Avoid famous ones.</strong> Overlord, Market Garden and Desert Storm already belong to history, and fictional agents belong to their films. The generator leaves them out.</li><li><strong>For a game or story,</strong> give the code name a reason: who chose it, and what they were thinking about that day.</li><li><strong>For a real product,</strong> a code name is usually internal. If it might end up on a website or a box, check it isn't already a product or a trade mark first.</li></ul><p>For a spy's team or agency, try the <a href=\"superhero-team-name-generator.html\">Superhero Team Name Generator</a>, or the <a href=\"gamertag-generator.html\">Gamertag Generator</a> for an online handle.</p>"
